│   ├── normalize.py
│   ├── offline.py
│   └── storage.py
└── tests/
```

## Setup

### PowerShell

```powershell
cd coryn-crawler
python -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
pip install -e .
Copy-Item .env.example .env
```

### Linux/macOS

```bash
cd coryn-crawler
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -e .
cp .env.example .env
```

## Recommended: create the full offline-ready dataset

```powershell
coryn-crawl full --offline-ready
```

This performs the complete workflow:

1. crawl all item, monster and map collections;
2. fetch/cache item details;
3. fetch/cache monster details and drops;
4. export normalized CSV tables;
5. visit item HTML pages and download item images;
6. calculate SHA-256, file size and image dimensions;
7. build `data/offline/` using only relative local asset paths.

To also create a portable ZIP:

```powershell
coryn-crawl full --offline-ready --offline-zip
```

The resulting archive is:

```text
data/coryn-offline-dataset.zip
```

If the crawl is interrupted, run the same command again. API details and successfully crawled images are reused.

## Offline output

```text
data/offline/
├── manifest.json
├── README.txt
├── data/
│   ├── items.csv
│   ├── items_with_images.csv
│   ├── items_with_images.jsonl
│   ├── item_stats.csv
│   ├── item_images.csv
│   ├── monsters.csv
│   ├── bosses.csv
│   ├── monster_drops.csv
│   ├── maps.csv
│   ├── map_monsters.csv
│   └── catalog.json
├── raw/
│   ├── items.jsonl
│   ├── items_details.jsonl
│   ├── monsters.jsonl
│   ├── monsters_details.jsonl
│   ├── maps.jsonl
│   └── item_images.jsonl
└── images/
    └── items/
        ├── 8571_reindeer_antlers.png
        └── ...
```

`items_with_images.csv` is the easiest table for an offline application. It contains:

```text
item_id
name
type_id
type_label
...
image_status
offline_image_path
image_sha256
image_file_size
image_width
image_height
image_content_type
source_page_url
source_image_url
```

Use `offline_image_path` for applications. For example:

```python
from pathlib import Path

OFFLINE_ROOT = Path("data/offline")
image_file = OFFLINE_ROOT / row["offline_image_path"]
```

`source_page_url` and `source_image_url` are preserved only as provenance. The offline dataset does **not** require those URLs to work.

## Manifest and integrity

`data/offline/manifest.json` records:

- whether the dataset is complete enough to be considered `offline_ready`;
- row counts;
- number of copied images;
- unindexed items;
- image crawl errors;
- downloaded-image records whose local files are missing;
- SHA-256 and byte size for each bundled file.

A bundle is marked `offline_ready: false` if, for example, the image crawl was only partially completed or an image previously marked as downloaded has disappeared locally.

## Crawl commands

Collection-only crawl:

```powershell
coryn-crawl crawl all
```

Complete API crawl without images:

```powershell
coryn-crawl full
```

Individual resources:

```powershell
coryn-crawl crawl items --details
coryn-crawl crawl monsters --details
coryn-crawl crawl maps
```

## Item images

Coryn's structured API does not provide the main item image, so the image crawler visits:

```text
item.php?id=<item_id>
```

and discovers image candidates from normal/lazy `<img>` tags, linked full-size images, `srcset`, OpenGraph/Twitter metadata, JSON-LD, CSS background images and image-like data attributes.

Run:

```powershell
coryn-crawl images
```

Smoke test only the first 20 items:

```powershell
coryn-crawl images --limit 20
```

The normal image index is:

```text
data/processed/item_images.csv
```

with fields:

```text
item_id,item_name,page_url,image_url,local_path,content_type,sha256,file_size,width,height,downloaded_at,status,error
```

Image crawling is resumable. `downloaded` and confirmed `no_image` results are reused; prior `error` results are retried.

## Build/rebuild offline bundle without crawling

If you have already crawled/exported everything:

```powershell
coryn-crawl offline
```

Or build it and ZIP it:

```powershell
coryn-crawl offline --zip
```

This command makes no network requests. It packages the local crawl you already have.

## Export normalized tables again

```powershell
coryn-crawl export
```

Detailed JSONL files are preferred automatically when available.

## Configuration

Edit `.env`:

```dotenv
CORYN_SITE_URL=https://coryn.club
CORYN_BASE_URL=https://coryn.club/api/v1
CORYN_PAGE_SIZE=100
CORYN_REQUEST_DELAY=0.30
CORYN_TIMEOUT=30
CORYN_MAX_RETRIES=5
CORYN_USER_AGENT=CorynResearchCrawler/0.3 (educational data collection; public API)
CORYN_OUTPUT_DIR=data
```

Keep a non-zero request delay. HTTP 429 responses are retried with backoff.

## Main normalized tables

- `items.csv`: one row per item.
- `item_stats.csv`: one row per item/stat pair.
- `monsters.csv`: one row per monster/difficulty record.
- `bosses.csv`: boss and mini-boss subset; do not deduplicate solely by name because modes/difficulties may differ.
- `monster_drops.csv`: monster → dropped item relationship.
- `maps.csv`: maps.
- `map_monsters.csv`: map → monster relationship.
- `item_images.csv`: source/local image index and integrity metadata.

## Tests

```powershell
pip install pytest
pytest -q
```

Tests are offline and use fake HTTP clients; they do not hit Coryn Club.

## Notes

- Uses public read-only data/pages only.
- No login scraping.
- No Selenium required.
- Raw API responses are preserved so future code can recover fields not represented by normalized CSVs.
- The offline bundle keeps source URLs only for provenance; local images and local tables are the canonical assets for offline use.
